---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ProjectFigure from '../../components/ProjectFigure.astro';
import ContributionTimeline from '../../components/ContributionTimeline.astro';
import { guessr, guessrImages } from '../../data/projects';
import { en as copy } from '../../i18n/en';
const locale = 'en';
---
<CaseStudyLayout locale={locale} copy={copy}>
  <Fragment slot="introduction">
    <p>I launched <strong>Mario Kart World Guessr</strong> on June 21, 2025, around two weeks after <em>Mario Kart World</em> was released on Nintendo Switch 2. I wanted to test a simple idea: use the game's open world and Photo Mode to create a <em>GeoGuessr</em>-style game.</p>
    <p>The project began as a small, frontend-only Vanilla JS application, built in under two weeks.</p>
    <p>More than a year later, following its success and player feedback, <strong>Mario Kart World Guessr</strong> has achievements, difficulty levels, several game modes, leaderboards, and a way for any player to submit their own photos. It now runs on a React frontend connected to a PHP API and a MariaDB database with around twenty tables…</p>
    <p>You could say this <strong>Mario Kart</strong> project got a little out of hand!</p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="idea">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><h2 id="idea">A game experience shaped by player feedback.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose"><p>The idea follows the same principle as <em>GeoGuessr</em>. A photo appears, and the player must place a marker on the <strong>Mario Kart World</strong> map to guess where it was taken. They then receive a score based on the distance from the actual location.</p><p>One of the first pieces of feedback I received was that the map was awkward to use; at the time, it was simply a displayed image.</p><p>I decided to improve the experience with <strong>React Leaflet</strong>, which lets players zoom into the map image and makes the game more comfortable for anyone who wants to be truly precise.</p></div>
  </section>
  <ProjectFigure image={guessrImages.result[locale]} alt="A round result showing the player's guess, the actual location and the score." caption="The game scores the player's guess based on its distance from the photo's actual location. It also shows where other players tend to place their guesses for that photo." pending={copy.labels.figurePending} />

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="community">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><h2 id="community">The game grows with its players.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose"><p><strong>Mario Kart World Guessr</strong> launched with my own photos. It quickly became clear that this would not be enough to give players a reason to return, so a photo submission system was the natural next step.</p><p>Technically, when a player submits a photo through the site, an <em>Issue</em> opens in the project's GitHub repository with the photo's details. I am automatically notified and can moderate the submission directly from GitHub. I simply close the <em>Issue</em> with the appropriate status to approve or reject the photo. Either way, the photographer receives a notification directly on the site.</p></div>
    <ContributionTimeline locale={locale} />
  </section>

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="albums">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><h2 id="albums">A game built around its community.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose"><p>One of my ambitions is to showcase the photographers who submit their work. They can create albums, select their favourite photos, and share them.</p><p>Players can also start a game of <strong>Mario Kart World Guessr</strong> using only the photos from a particular album, making it perfect for preparing custom games for friends!</p></div>
  </section>
  <ProjectFigure image={guessrImages.album[locale]} alt="A photographer's album showing a selection of their Mario Kart World photos." caption="Albums let photographers choose and share their favourite photos." pending={copy.labels.figurePending} narrow />

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="daily">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><h2 id="daily">Something to come back to.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose"><p>Over time, I added several game modes, leaderboards, achievements, and unlockable characters.</p><p>The daily challenge automatically selects {guessr.dailyPhotos} different photos each day. It gives players a fresh selection to explore, without requiring me to curate a new set every morning.</p></div>
  </section>
  <ProjectFigure image={guessrImages.daily[locale]} alt="The daily photo challenge in Mario Kart World Guessr." caption={`The daily challenge: ${guessr.dailyPhotos} different photos, automatically selected each day.`} pending={copy.labels.figurePending} narrow />

  <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="beyond">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><h2 id="beyond">A game that crossed borders.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose"><p>The game found an audience beyond France. I have seen videos of American, German, and even Japanese players taking an interest in it.<br />Coming across a Japanese video of someone playing a game I developed is a strange experience!</p></div>
    <ProjectFigure image={guessrImages.videos} alt="A selection of videos from international players featuring Mario Kart World Guessr." pending={copy.labels.figurePending} narrow>
      <Fragment slot="caption">Many YouTubers have made one or more videos about <strong>Mario Kart World Guessr</strong>.</Fragment>
    </ProjectFigure>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-takeaway" aria-labelledby="learned">
    <div class="case-section-heading"><h2 id="learned">From personal project to real product.</h2></div>
    <div class="prose"><p><strong>Mario Kart World Guessr</strong> is probably the personal project of mine that feels most like a real product.</p><p>At first, decisions mostly came from what I wanted to build. Now they also come from usage, contributions, feedback, and what I notice when I watch people play.</p></div>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
